#{[17592186445609 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445609 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445609 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445609 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445609 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "32d4b06d-f6f3-5351-bf77-6d5871dae39c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445609 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 6{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445610 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445610 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334765" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445610 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445610 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445610 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4daa880b-394e-5da4-a6f8-c7b8b600dc37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445610 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445611 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445611 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335269" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445611 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445611 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=8x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=8y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{8t} & t{e}^{8t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{8t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}].$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445611 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5d3d32e6-9123-5862-a54d-5cd0459cabee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445611 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{8t} & t{e}^{8t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{8t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{8t} & t{e}^{8t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{8t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5t{e}^{8t} \\\\ 5{e}^{8t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445612 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445612 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445612 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445612 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336478" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445612 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445612 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(0)=c$  \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,x(0)$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from the definition of ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ as the standard fundamental matrix by  \n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by  \n{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445612 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ee364d4-3865-58d1-b13b-994e56af1ccf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445612 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Starting from\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwe evaluate both sides at $t=0$ to obtain\n\n$$\nx(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c,\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L3}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{evaluating both sides at }t=0.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ By definition, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ satisfies the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nThe second condition here is exactly line $\\text{L4},$ so $\\text{L4}$ follows from the definition of ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ as the standard fundamental matrix by $\\text{using the initial condition }{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$, $\\text{L4},$ and $\\text{L5}.$ From $\\text{L3}$ we know\n\n$$\nx(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c,\n$$\n\nand from $\\text{L4}$ we have ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.$ Substituting ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$ into $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c$ gives\n\n$$\nx(0)={I}_{n}c=c,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly line $\\text{L5}.$ Thus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{substituting }{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}\\text{ into }x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445613 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445613 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334778" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445613 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445613 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445613 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "964cfe5a-540f-5a2a-90eb-038dd13a228b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445613 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445614 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445614 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445614 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445614 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337108" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445614 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445614 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,c$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $x({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ by {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ can be obtained by {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L9}$ follows from $\\text{L8}$ by the fact that {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445614 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c3c2f127-8fe3-52c5-8ea9-fa818612f56c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445614 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1},$ $\\text{L2},$ and $\\text{L3}.$ From $\\text{L2},$ we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c.\n$$\n\nUsing $\\text{L1}$, we substitute ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ into this equation to obtain\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L3}.$ Thus, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{substituting }{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}\\text{ into }x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L7}.$ Using line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,c.\n$$\n\nFrom line $\\text{L6}$, we know that $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}).$ Substituting this expression for $c$ into the formula for $x(t)$ gives\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L7}.$ Thus, line $\\text{L7}$ can be obtained by $\\text{substituting }c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\text{ into }x(t)={e}^{At}c.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L8}$ and $\\text{L9}.$ Starting from $\\text{L8},$\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwe rewrite the exponent using the algebraic identity $At-A{t}_{0}=A(t-{t}_{0})$ to get\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L9}.$ Thus, $\\text{L9}$ follows from $\\text{L8}$ by the fact that $At-A{t}_{0}=A(t-{t}_{0})\\text{ for any }t\\text{ and }{t}_{0}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445615 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445615 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445615 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335427" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445615 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445615 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(3) \\\\ y(3)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445615 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e674cbe1-254a-5d93-a9b4-be22a2029aaf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445615 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-3)}\\,x(3) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4(t-3)} & (t-3){e}^{4(t-3)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-3)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,-{e}^{4(t-3)}\\, \\\\ \\,0\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
